\noindent \textbf{Ablation Studies.}
We conduct ablation analysis on the BUS dataset using 5-fold cross-validation, as IU X-ray already exhibits near-ceiling diagnostic accuracy (ACC > 0.96), leaving limited headroom for meaningful comparison against conventional vision backbones. 
As shown in Table~\ref{tab:vqa}, among conventional vision backbones, Swin achieves the strongest performance (0.7497), outperforming ResNet and ViT variants. 
For multimodal models, Qwen2.5 attains 0.7447 under direct prompting, while enabling thinking improves performance to 0.7779. 
Similarly, Qwen3 reaches 0.7810, indicating that reasoning-oriented generation enhances classification reliability. 
CORAL further improves over these baselines: Qwen2.5-CORAL achieves 0.7951, and Qwen3-CORAL attains the best overall result of 0.8017, surpassing both conventional vision backbones and multimodal models with thinking. 
These results suggest that structured concept grounding combined with spatially guided feature modulation provides complementary gains beyond pure visual training or reasoning-based prompting alone.
\noindent \textbf{Case Study.}
Fig.~\ref{fig:case} presents a qualitative comparison on BUS using Qwen. 
Under direct prompting with the raw image, the model generates inconsistent attribute descriptions and predicts BI-RADS 4C, resulting in an overestimation of malignancy risk despite the correct outcome.
Providing the ground-truth mask improves spatial focus and corrects some morphological cues, but attribute reasoning remains inconsistent, leading to an incorrect diagnosis.
In contrast, CORAL couples mask-guided feature modulation with concept supervision, anchoring attribute prediction (e.g., non-parallel orientation, circumscribed margin, round shape) to the localized lesion region. This structured attribute grounding leads to a correct BI-RADS 4A assessment with a malignant conclusion consistent with the ground truth.
